        "title": "Explain normalization?",
        "category": "Database Design & Normalization",
        "language": "sql",
        "explanation": "Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity by dividing large tables into smaller, related tables and defining relationships between them.\\n\\nNormalization is a systematic approach to decomposing database tables to eliminate data redundancy and dependency anomalies. Introduced by Edgar F. Codd, it follows a series of rules called normal forms. The core principle is that each piece of data should be stored in only one place, which prevents inconsistencies when data is updated, inserted, or deleted. Without normalization, a database suffers from three types of anomalies: Insertion Anomaly (unable to insert data without some other unrelated data), Deletion Anomaly (deleting one piece of data unintentionally removes other data), and Update Anomaly (updating one field requires updating multiple rows). Normalization addresses these by splitting tables and establishing foreign key relationships. In practice, most enterprise applications normalize to the Third Normal Form (3NF), while data warehouses often use denormalized schemas for read performance.",
        "example": "In an e-commerce platform, storing customer details in every order row causes update anomalies. By normalizing into Customers, Products, Orders, and OrderDetails tables, each piece of data exists in exactly one place. Changing a customer email requires updating just one row in the Customers table.",
        "code": "-- Before Normalization: Unnormalized Orders Table\nCREATE TABLE Orders_Unnormalized (\n    OrderID INT, CustomerName VARCHAR(100), CustomerEmail VARCHAR(100),\n    CustomerCity VARCHAR(50), ProductName VARCHAR(100),\n    ProductPrice DECIMAL(10,2), Quantity INT, OrderDate DATE\n);\n\n-- After Normalization: Split into related tables\nCREATE TABLE Customers (\n    CustomerID INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY,\n    CustomerName VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    CustomerEmail VARCHAR(100), CustomerCity VARCHAR(50)\n);\nCREATE TABLE Products (\n    ProductID INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY,\n    ProductName VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    ProductPrice DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE TABLE Orders (\n    OrderID INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY,\n    CustomerID INT FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES Customers(CustomerID),\n    OrderDate DATE DEFAULT GETDATE()\n);\nCREATE TABLE OrderDetails (\n    OrderDetailID INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY,\n    OrderID INT FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES Orders(OrderID),\n    ProductID INT FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES Products(ProductID),\n    Quantity INT NOT NULL\n);",
