        "explanation": "Triggers are special stored procedures that automatically execute in response to DML events (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on a table. They are needed for enforcing complex business rules, audit logging, and maintaining data integrity that cannot be handled by constraints alone.\\n\\nTriggers are special stored procedures that fire automatically in response to DML events (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on a table. They enforce complex business rules, audit data changes, maintain derived data, and implement cascading actions that constraints cannot handle. Triggers run within the same transaction as the triggering statement, so a trigger failure rolls back the entire operation.",
        "example": "This audit trigger captures all changes to the Employees table by joining the inserted and deleted virtual tables. INSERT has only inserted rows, DELETE has only deleted rows, and UPDATE has both.",
        "code": "CREATE TRIGGER trg_AuditEmployeeChanges\nON Employees\nAFTER INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE AS\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO EmployeeAudit (Action, EmployeeID, ChangedBy, ChangedDate)\n    SELECT CASE WHEN i.EmployeeID IS NOT NULL AND d.EmployeeID IS NULL THEN 'INSERT'\n                WHEN i.EmployeeID IS NOT NULL AND d.EmployeeID IS NOT NULL THEN 'UPDATE'\n                ELSE 'DELETE' END,\n           ISNULL(i.EmployeeID, d.EmployeeID), SUSER_NAME(), GETDATE()\n    FROM inserted i FULL JOIN deleted d ON i.EmployeeID = d.EmployeeID;\nEND;",
        "tip": "A trigger is like a motion-sensor light - it turns on automatically when someone enters the room. You do not have to flip the switch yourself. Similarly, a trigger runs automatically when data changes, without anyone explicitly calling it.",
        "mistake": "- Overusing triggers for simple validation (use constraints)\\n- Not considering trigger performance impact\\n- Creating nested trigger chains\\n- Forgetting triggers fire for each statement, not each row\\n- Not handling multi-row operations in triggers",
        "summary": "Triggers are special stored procedures that automatically execute in response to DML events (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on a table. They are needed for enforcing complex business rules, audit logging, and maintaining data integrity that cannot be handled by constraints alone."
