        "explanation": "SQL Server supports DML Triggers (AFTER and INSTEAD OF) that fire on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE operations, and DDL Triggers that fire on database-level events like CREATE, ALTER, DROP. Logon Triggers fire on login events.\\n\\nSQL Server supports DML Triggers (AFTER and INSTEAD OF) for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, DDL Triggers for CREATE/ALTER/DROP events, and Logon Triggers for login events. AFTER triggers fire after the DML completes. INSTEAD OF triggers replace the DML operation. DDL triggers operate at database or server level for auditing schema changes. Logon triggers fire after authentication but before the session starts.",
        "example": "AFTER trigger logs all new orders. INSTEAD OF trigger prevents deletions by replacing the DELETE. DDL trigger captures schema changes with the SQL command that was executed.",
        "code": "-- AFTER Trigger (auditing)\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_AuditOrders ON Orders AFTER INSERT AS\nBEGIN INSERT INTO OrderLog SELECT * FROM inserted; END;\n-- INSTEAD OF Trigger (prevent delete)\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_PreventDelete ON Orders INSTEAD OF DELETE AS\nBEGIN RAISERROR('Deletes not allowed', 16, 1); END;\n-- DDL Trigger (audit schema changes)\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_AuditDDL ON DATABASE FOR CREATE_TABLE, ALTER_TABLE, DROP_TABLE AS\nBEGIN INSERT INTO SchemaAudit VALUES (EVENTDATA().value('(/EVENT_INSTANCE/TSQLCommand/CommandText)[1]','nvarchar(max)'), GETDATE()); END;",
        "tip": "AFTER trigger is like a security camera that records what already happened. INSTEAD OF trigger is like a bouncer who stops someone at the door and decides what to do instead. DDL trigger watches the building structure being changed.",
        "mistake": "- Using AFTER trigger to prevent an operation (too late)\\n- Not knowing INSTEAD OF works on views\\n- Forgetting DDL triggers exist\\n- Confusing trigger scope (table vs database)",
        "summary": "SQL Server supports DML Triggers (AFTER and INSTEAD OF) that fire on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE operations, and DDL Triggers that fire on database-level events like CREATE, ALTER, DROP. Logon Triggers fire on login events."
