        "language": "sql",
        "explanation": "A transaction is a logical unit of work that consists of one or more database operations, ensuring all operations complete successfully (COMMIT) or none at all (ROLLBACK). Implement using BEGIN TRANSACTION, COMMIT TRANSACTION, and ROLLBACK TRANSACTION statements with proper error handling.\\n\\nA transaction is a logical unit of work ensuring all operations complete successfully (COMMIT) or none at all (ROLLBACK). Implement using BEGIN TRANSACTION, COMMIT TRANSACTION, and ROLLBACK TRANSACTION. Transactions enforce ACID properties. In SQL Server, auto-commit mode is default (each statement is a transaction). Explicit transactions are needed for multi-statement operations. Always include error handling with TRY-CATCH to ensure proper rollback.",
        "example": "This bank transfer wraps two UPDATEs in a transaction. If either fails, both are rolled back ensuring money is not lost. The TRY-CATCH ensures proper rollback on errors.",
        "code": "BEGIN TRY\n    BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 500 WHERE AccountID = 1;\n    UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 500 WHERE AccountID = 2;\n    COMMIT TRANSACTION;\n    PRINT 'Transfer successful';\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n    IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0\n        ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;\n    PRINT 'Transfer failed: ' + ERROR_MESSAGE();\nEND CATCH;",
        "tip": "A transaction is like ordering at a restaurant - either the entire meal is prepared and served, or if something cannot be made, the whole order is cancelled. You cannot get just the appetizer if the main course is unavailable.",
